          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 12 — How Nature Works in Harmony"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.886,
            0.029,
            0.923,
            0.044
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "207"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.085,
            0.776,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Habitats have biotic components (plants, animals, microbes) and abiotic components (air, water, soil, temperature)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.122,
            0.775,
            0.157
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The interaction between biotic components and abiotic components in an area forms an ecosystem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.158,
            0.775,
            0.193
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ecosystems can be terrestrial (forests, grasslands, deserts) or aquatic (ponds, lakes, sea, oceans)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.195,
            0.775,
            0.246
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Organisms are often classified as producers (plants), consumers (herbivores, carnivores, omnivores), and decomposers (bacteria, fungi)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.248,
            0.775,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Producers make their own food, while consumers eat plants or animals. Decomposers break down dead matter and recycle nutrients."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.3,
            0.775,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Food chains depict who eats whom in an ecosystem, and food webs show how these chains are interconnected. The positions that different organisms occupy in a food chain are called trophic levels."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.354,
            0.775,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Some organisms live in relationships, like mutualism (both benefit), commensalism (one benefits, other is unaffected), and parasitism (one benefits, one is harmed)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.407,
            0.775,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The benefits that ecosystems offer are crucial for human survival and well-being. They provide clean air, water, food, medicine, and climate regulation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.46,
            0.776,
            0.531
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Human activities like pollution, deforestation, habitat loss, climate change, invasive species, and overexploitation of natural resources threaten ecosystems. Protecting them through efforts, such as conservation like national parks and sanctuaries is vital."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.554,
            0.427,
            0.575
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Keep the curiosity alive"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.593,
            0.743,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Refer to the given diagram (Fig. 12.19) and select the wrong statement."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.636,
            0.602,
            0.657
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) A community is larger than a population."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.661,
            0.622,
            0.682
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) A community is smaller than an ecosystem."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.686,
            0.565,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) An ecosystem is part of a community."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.764,
            0.558,
            0.939,
            0.76
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
